---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Context Strategies - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="development" subChapterId="context-strategies">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Every time you interact with an AI coding tool, you're working inside a context window: a fixed amount of text the model can "see" at once.
				Everything in your conversation, every file it reads, every error it encounters, every response it gives, all of it fills that window.
				Frontier models have grown large, with the current leaders (Claude Opus 4.8, GPT-5.5, Gemini 3.x) offering 1M tokens or more.
				But the window is still finite, and tools like Claude Code and Cursor start compressing your conversation before you reach the edge.
				When that happens, things start getting lost. As you'll see below, a big window is not the safety net it looks like.
			</p>

			<h3>Start Fresh Between Tasks</h3>

			<p>
				<strong>Start a new conversation for each distinct task</strong>.
				When you finish implementing a feature and move on to fixing a bug, open a new chat. When you're done with the bug and want to write tests,
				open another one. Each conversation should have one clear purpose.
			</p>

			<p>
				As a conversation grows, the AI's understanding of what you're doing becomes diluted. Early instructions fade.
				Context from a previous task bleeds into the current one. The AI might try to "fix" something it saw three tasks ago, or apply a pattern
				from an unrelated change. A fresh conversation gives the AI a clean slate focused entirely on the task at hand.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Practical tip">
				<p>
					A good rule of thumb: if you'd create a separate Git commit for it, it should be a separate conversation.
					Commit your work, then start fresh. If you have your requirements or plans broken into multiple tasks, start a new conversation for each task.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>Context Windows Fill Up Fast</h3>

			<p>
				Most people underestimate how quickly the context window fills. Your prompts are only a small piece of it. The bulk comes from{" "}
				<strong>tool activity</strong>: every file the AI reads, every command it runs, every search result it processes. A single file read can
				consume thousands of tokens. Ask the AI to explore a few files, run a build, and read the error output, and you've used a significant
				chunk of your window before any real work has started.
			</p>

			<p>
				When the window gets too full, tools like Claude Code and Cursor will <strong>compress</strong> earlier parts of the conversation.
				This means the AI creates a summary of what happened and discards the original details. In theory this preserves the important bits.
				In practice, nuance gets lost. That specific edge case you mentioned early on? The particular naming convention you asked it to follow?
				The subtle constraint in your requirements? These details tend to get compressed away.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Watch for these signs">
				<p>
					If the AI starts contradicting earlier instructions, re-reading files it already saw, or "forgetting" constraints you clearly stated,
					your context has likely been compressed. This is your signal to commit what you have and start a new conversation.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>A Bigger Window Isn't the Answer</h3>

			<p>
				It's tempting to look at a 1M-token window and assume you can stop worrying about context. You can't. Models don't use their
				context uniformly, and their reliability drops as the window fills, well before it's full. Chroma's 2025{" "}
				<a href="https://research.trychroma.com/context-rot" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">context rot study</a>{" "}
				tested 18 frontier models across Claude, GPT, and Gemini and found every one of them degraded as input grew, even on simple retrieval tasks.
				A model's <em>effective</em> context is often a fraction of its advertised maximum.
			</p>

			<p>
				This is the key thing to internalize: the goal isn't to fit as much as possible into a huge window, it's to keep what the AI sees
				focused and relevant. A short, clean conversation outperforms a long, cluttered one, even when both are nowhere near the limit. That's
				why the strategies on this page matter more as windows get bigger, not less.
			</p>

			<h3>Recency Bias</h3>

			<p>
				Even before compression kicks in, AI models naturally give more weight to what appeared recently in the conversation (and somewhat what was said at the beginning of the conversation).
				Instructions you gave at the start lose influence as new messages pile on top. It's how
				attention mechanisms work in these models.
			</p>

			<p>
				If something matters, re-state it when it's relevant. When you're about to start a step that
				touches the database, remind the AI of your constraint right then. Don't rely on it remembering something from the top of a
				long conversation.
			</p>

			<h3>Reference Files</h3>

			<p>
				When you need the AI to work with a file, reference it by path rather than copying it, or just describing its contents.
				Tools like Claude Code and Cursor can read files on demand.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Instead of this</div>
					<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
						hey can you update the user model to require a phone number?
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Do this</div>
					<p class="font-mono text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed">
						"Look at src/models/user.ts and update the validation to require a phone number."
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				The same logic applies to requirements documents, tickets, and any other reference material. If it's accessible as a file
				or through an integration (like Confluence or Jira), point the AI to the source rather than pasting the text. The AI can
				pull in exactly what it needs, when it needs it.
			</p>

			<h3>Clean Up Noisy Linters and Test Output</h3>

			<p>
				One often overlooked source of context bloat is verbose output from linters and test runners. If your build spits out
				hundreds of warnings, or your test suite logs walls of deprecation notices, all of that flows into the context window
				every time the AI runs those commands.
			</p>

			<p>
				Fixing these warnings is a double win. Your project gets healthier (warnings exist for a reason), and the AI gets
				cleaner output from tools. If you can't fix them all right away, consider configuring your
				linter or test runner to suppress known warnings so the output stays concise.
			</p>

			<h3>Planning Modes</h3>

			<p>
				The tools themselves are converging on the same approach. Many AI coding tools have started adopting a pattern where they
				generate a detailed plan, then <strong>clear the context and restart from just the plan</strong>. They do this because
				a focused, clean context produces better results than a long, cluttered one.
			</p>

			<p>
				Sub-agents follow the same idea from the other direction: you hand off a big exploration (say, "figure out how auth works across these ten files")
				to a separate agent that burns through its own context and hands back only a short summary, keeping your main conversation clean.
			</p>

			<p>
				Skills and custom instructions (like CLAUDE.md files or Cursor rules) work on the same principle. Instead of explaining your project
				conventions in every conversation, you write them once in a file. The AI reads them fresh at the start of each session, right when
				they're most prominent in the context. No compression, no recency bias pushing them down.
			</p>

			<p>
				The strategies on this page are about doing manually what these tools are trying to automate: keeping the context
				focused, relevant, and clean. Start fresh often. Let the AI read things on its own schedule. Re-state what matters when it matters.
			</p>

			<h3>Further Reading</h3>
			<ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 my-4">
				<li>
					<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Effective context engineering for AI agents</a> — Anthropic on treating context as a finite resource (Sept 2025)
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://research.trychroma.com/context-rot" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Context Rot: How Increasing Input Tokens Impacts LLM Performance</a> — Chroma's study across 18 frontier models (July 2025)
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Context windows</a> — Anthropic docs on how the window works and current model sizes
				</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
